Subject: Release the shape a polymorphic cache way held when the way is replaced

What this does
  When all four ways of a site's polymorphic cache are full and a fifth shape is
  filled, the replaced way's shape reference is released before the new shape is
  stored.

Why we need it
  The fill duplicated the new shape and overwrote the way without releasing the old
  one, so every replacement leaked a shape reference (and with it the shape's whole
  object graph until runtime teardown, which then failed its "no objects left"
  check). Nothing used a fifth shape until compiled bodies started serving
  polymorphic sites. The release is a plain reference drop: the finalizers it can reach
  take only the runtime (no context), so none can run JS; the same drop already runs
  inline in compiled stores.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -62035,8 +62035,9 @@
     }
     e = b->aot_poly + (size_t)site * AOT_POLY_WAYS;
     sh = JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(obj)->shape;
-    /* The entries hold weak shape pointers, so every hit is revalidated by the
-       caller's shape compare against a live object; nothing is released. */
+    /* The entries hold a reference to their shape; every hit is revalidated by the
+       caller's shape compare against a live object. The reference is released when
+       a way is replaced and at teardown. */
     for (k = 0; k < AOT_POLY_WAYS; k++) {
         if (e[k].shape == sh) {
             e[k].off = off2;
@@ -62064,6 +62065,10 @@
        by the engine's own paths before the next attempt. */
     if (!sh->is_hashed)
         return 0;
+    /* Replacing a full way releases the shape the way held, or it leaks (and keeps
+       its whole object graph alive until teardown). */
+    if (e[slot].shape != NULL)
+        js_free_shape(ctx->rt, e[slot].shape);
     e[slot].shape = js_dup_shape(sh);
     e[slot].off = off2;
     e[slot].kind = (uint16_t)(write ? AOT_POLY_WRITE : AOT_POLY_READ);
